From 16023aa1885dd94a1e33eef63cfab66a115365d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 16:49:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 19/23] feat(wallpaper): native widgets, aggregate crawl,
 tag/category filters in OCS browser
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Reworks WallpaperOcsBrowser against three operator complaints:

1. Not native UX. The picker previously opened with two bare Gtk.DropDowns
   sitting in a plain Box next to two unlabeled Labels. Nothing about the
   layout suggested there was more than one online wallpaper source, or
   what a category even was. Now matches the Singularity settings surface:
   provider choice is a SelectionRow inside a PreferencesGroup -- the same
   pattern desktop_page.vala uses for Wallpaper Source. Filter rows are
   raw Singularity.Widgets.Chip widgets in a FlowBox (a single ChipBar is
   single-active, but the tag filter needs multi-select AND across many
   tags -- tracking the active set and toggling chip.active ourselves
   scales to arbitrarily many tags without fighting ChipBar.set_active).

2. Hard to discover sources / category is a hard prerequisite. Category is
   no longer a gate on loading -- it is a post-load filter over an
   already-loaded set. The grid is populated up front by an aggregate
   crawl that pulls page 1 from every usable category under the selected
   provider in parallel (CRAWL_WORKERS=4), bounded by an overall item cap
   (CRAWL_ITEM_CAP=1500) and per-category subprocess timeout
   (CRAWL_CATEGORY_TIMEOUT=60s, matching the prior single-call bound).
   Progress is live ("Loaded K of N categories · M wallpapers so far"),
   not a silent spinner. The existing Cancellable cuts the rest of the
   crawl off cleanly when the user closes the window or switches
   provider; mid-crawl provider changes bump a generation counter and
   the workers drop out without racing the new crawl.

Tags are surfaced as a second chip row built live from the union of
tags actually present across loaded items -- never hardcoded, rebuilt as
new categories stream in. Tag chip activation toggles the tag in/out of
an AND-set; the existing free-text search composes with both filters
(text AND active category AND every active tag), AND instead of
replacing them.

The visible close (x) on each chip is a widget-rendering fact of
libsingularity Chip that cannot be suppressed. It is wired to a no-op
for filter chips (clicking x does nothing visible) and on card-level
tag chips (clicking x does nothing visible). Using it to do the same
thing as activated would read as remove this tag from existence -- the
worse failure mode. Wrote it that way on purpose.

3. Tags were dropped on the floor. The companion parser commit adds
   WallpaperOcsItem.tags; this one surfaces them as the second filter
   chip row above and as a small tag-chip strip on each card so the
   user can see what tags an item carries without expanding it.

Import flow (import_card, WallpaperOcsImports) is unchanged -- that
part was out of scope and is not broken. The thumbnail streaming
logic and the Cancel-on-close wiring are unchanged.
